m we  i'd say for us in astrazeneca today  single cell sequencing is a technology  that we are exploring  rather than being integrated into any  specific project but absolutely take the  point  of the questioner um you know we we do  from our own data and it's obviously  common in the literature um we see you  know very significant differences across  singles  across different cell types uh within a  tissue  when we move into single cell rna  sequencing  okay so the next question uh is also  another big picture question  um it was asked early on uh while you  were presenting and it  is what is the next revolutionary  technology that you believe will turn  science into medicines now that's a very  good question this is a can you predict  the future question  um what is the next revolutionary  technology  um so okay so  so something that we are incredibly  excited about and also others in the  field are incredibly excited about  is the application of artificial  intelligence and machine learning in  chemistry  um and this is probably the area that  excites me  most so using  uh artificial intelligence and chemistry  we're asked two questions  um we asked two questions first first  question is  can we use ai to tell us which molecule  to make next  and then can we use ai to tell us the  best route of synthesis to make that  molecule  and we know that the answers to both of  those questions are yes  what we don't know is whether we can do  that to dramatically shorten  the timelines from from the  identification of initial hit  through to the uh delivery of a drug  candidate  my prediction our prediction is over the  next five years  that we will be able to reduce the  timelines from identification to  candidate selection from the current  three to four years  to maybe less than 12 months through  applying ai in chemistry  and we're also looking applying similar  ai in our antibody optimization it's not  just simply unique to  um small molecule discovery but this  technology will  hopefully dramatically decrease cycle  time in terms of our ability to get  medicines to patients quickly  but also increase success in in through  allowing us to identify  uh molecules deliver clinical candidates  for those projects that might have  failed  okay wow lots of great questions that's  a great question that's probably the  best set of quite  congratulate everybody on the call  that's probably the best set of  questions i've ever been asked in one of  these  presentations yeah a lot a lot of big  thinkers out there today for  for this talk so i would like to thank  you  steve and jean for your presentations  and  i would like to thank everybody for  joining us today for this presentation  we ask that you complete a short survey  at the close of this webinar  your thoughts and comments would be  greatly appreciated especially  from such big thinkers that we have on  here  make sure you visit the poster hall and  exhibit booths where you can learn more  about a variety of exciting technologies  that we have  and this webcast can be viewed on demand  through july of 2021  until next time thank you very much and  have a great day
 

